steps.body:
In a sorted array, two pointers find a pair with a given sum in one pass. Start `left` at the first index and `right` at the last. If the pair's sum is too small, the only way up is a bigger left value, so move `left` right. If it's too big, move `right` left. Every step rules one value out for good, so the loop runs at most n times instead of trying every pair:

```java
int[] nums = {1, 3, 4, 6, 9};
int target = 13;
int left = 0;
int right = nums.length - 1;
while (left < right) {
    int sum = nums[left] + nums[right];
    if (sum == target) {
        break;
    } else if (sum < target) {
        left++;                  // need a bigger sum
    } else {
        right--;                 // need a smaller sum
    }
}
System.out.println(nums[left] + " + " + nums[right]); // 4 + 9
```

`while (left < right)` stops before the pointers meet, so a value is never paired with itself. If the loop ends without a match, there is no pair, so put the search in a method that returns as soon as it finds one and returns a "none" value after the loop.

This only works on sorted data. On unsorted input, sort it first, or keep a `HashMap` of the values you have already seen.

example.code:
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int[] nums = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            nums[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }
        int q = sc.nextInt();
        for (int t = 0; t < q; t++) {
            int target = sc.nextInt();
            System.out.println(target + ": " + findPair(nums, target));
        }
    }

    static String findPair(int[] nums, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.length - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            int sum = nums[left] + nums[right];
            if (sum == target) {
                return (left + 1) + " and " + (right + 1);
            } else if (sum < target) {
                left++;
            } else {
                right--;
            }
        }
        return "none";
    }
}

