steps.body:
A window is a run of `k` neighbors, `nums[i]` to `nums[i + k - 1]`. Adding up every window from scratch costs k steps each. Sliding is cheaper: when the window moves one place right, one value enters and one leaves, so update the sum instead of redoing it:

```java
int[] sales = {4, 2, 7, 1, 8, 3};
int k = 3;
int window = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
    window += sales[i];                          // the first window
}
int best = window;
for (int right = k; right < sales.length; right++) {
    window += sales[right] - sales[right - k];   // one in, one out
    best = Math.max(best, window);
}
System.out.println(best); // 16, from 7 + 1 + 8
```

The value leaving is `sales[right - k]`, exactly k places behind the one entering. The window that ends at `right` starts at `right - k + 1`, which is how you report where the best window is.

Make sure `k` is at most the array's length before you build the first window, or that first loop reads past the end and throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`.

example.code:
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int[] sales = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            sales[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }
        int k = sc.nextInt();

        int window = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            window += sales[i];
        }
        StringBuilder sums = new StringBuilder("Windows: " + window);
        int best = window;
        int bestStart = 0;
        for (int right = k; right < n; right++) {
            window += sales[right] - sales[right - k];
            sums.append(' ').append(window);
            if (window > best) {
                best = window;
                bestStart = right - k + 1;
            }
        }
        System.out.println(sums);
        System.out.println("Best: " + best + " starting on day " + (bestStart + 1));
    }
}

